ntityx9ni:///sha-256;IUEoSXzqpSz2jXXbTx8mc-2-hDturN0WF9usQLOq9tcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;3ADSg7zkQwjFzLUlsie509Zgte6h6fm3sII_KN0VFL8ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bNd3ANcwVGGO58adFccpvwQxQpH5BUAhL8FE16xNV6Aƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkdTextƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;d2yYoR0hZKu7JLe3h8lqIHUHHfEDJBrUZZGzK0cwigQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkqSyndicationSourceƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;avIwyHhVkpsp9A30NR8xB9y8KQlOVdZRxtaYIcqxt9Mƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$urn:rmn:predicate:source-reports-urlƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;EMrgOCh-V2FCH8iaAkjiSzRXDE87MeX-g6AB3jAPBu4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarktUnsaturatedPredicateƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;mSgi4SdYlGJ6YWaG0Di-x6fbQHRmcMDp4Rsra-FJYawƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarksGroundedPredicationƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Z-ym5DpxyhI7vPr_DhHlqXgkAT21SjeiwUBVg_dZaq0ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;fI8QJryKq__0G5MCS41GKwIZY99k4ePaVREW_jxRpPYƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;kv-V8BDTxLfEnmlkkiMEzmG9zzRrVcRfUR6VHoh5IU8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-20T23:57:20.601Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;vgeygy1Vz-rYQt9pD_eoDAznUTTOzQaZhjzkaAaVL-gƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx^https://www.carbonbrief.org/qa-europes-may-and-june-heatwave-deaths-and-how-they-were-counted/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jomeCqApKUlmeUApoTbxc5e02cWr0SMsiHalcioE7z4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;LxBcTytX7N_ZMe8Ov6IuWI-gFhF5NE6cSPOF2NGBVFgƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;4TSyo61wBmXJxXOLPPxLlWrJ6RD4skLdt5MPybdh4wYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx^https://www.carbonbrief.org/qa-europes-may-and-june-heatwave-deaths-and-how-they-were-counted/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;J183uornmMHKtLsqwqMTIJvdnd64VMEYPM1Sv1N6SZkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyP&Recent weeks have seen a flurry of reports from public health authorities and scientists that estimate the deaths caused by Europeâ€™s record-breaking summer heatwaves .&nbsp; In France , the national public health agency reported 2,025 excess deaths over the week where the heatwave peaked in June.&nbsp; Authorities in Germany and Netherlands put the excess death toll during the same seven-day period at 5,753 and 533, respectively. An analysis from climate scientists in Carbon Brief found that France saw more than 2,700 heat-related deaths over 17 days in June. Separate research estimated there had been 2,700 heat-related deaths in the UKâ€™s May and June heatwaves â€“ 42% of which had been caused by human-caused climate change. There are a number of methods for how academics and governments tally deaths caused by extreme heat, each with their own advantages and drawbacks. Here, Carbon Brief looks at the different ways scientists and public health authorities have calculated the death toll of Europeâ€™s record-breaking summer heat.&nbsp; How established is the science of calculating heat deaths? What are the different approaches to counting heat deaths? What do the latest figures show for Europeâ€™s May and June heatwaves? What are the pros and cons of the â€˜excess deathsâ€™ method? What are the pros and cons of the â€˜statistical modellingâ€™ method? How established is the science of calculating heat deaths? Economists and epidemiologists have been studying the relationship between heat and mortality for nearly a century .&nbsp; A pioneering study published in 1923 by geographer Ellsworth Huntington and economist Margaret Justin that looked at mortality data for New York City over 1882-88 found that deaths increased rapidly as temperatures rose above 17C.&nbsp; As global temperatures have risen in response to human-caused carbon emissions, scientists have increasingly sought to understand how warming could impact mortality.&nbsp; The study of mortality caused by specific heatwave events dates back a few decades, with a 1995 heatwave in Chicago among the earliest events to be studied in detail. Over the past decade, a growing number of studies have gone a step further, by estimating the number of deaths caused by a specific heatwave event and then attributing a percentage or number of those deaths to human-caused climate change.&nbsp; Carbon Brief covered the first study of this type, which was published in Environmental Research Letters in 2016 and focused on a 2003 summer heatwave that caused tens of thousands of deaths across Europe. The study estimated that 506 of the 735 summer fatalities in Paris and 64 of the 315 in London were a result of human influence on the climate. More recently, a study in Climatic Change found that 27% of deaths in a 2018 heatwave in Zurich, Switzerland were linked to human-caused climate change and a paper in Science Advances estimated that 11-15% of deaths in a 2021 heatwave in British Columbia were attributable to global warming.&nbsp; Dr Christopher Callahan , assistant professor at the Oâ€™Neill School of Public and Environmental Affairs at Indiana University , tells Carbon Brief this type of â€œtwo-stepâ€ study has â€œreally explodedâ€ in recent years: â€œIt is really only in the last five to 10 years that we have seen this, partly because it does require interdisciplinary expertise. You need people who know how to run the epidemiological models and you need a climate analysis of the counterfactual [world] without climate change, which is its own effort.â€&nbsp; What are the different approaches to counting heat deaths? A central challenge in estimating deaths from a heatwave is that heat is rarely recorded as the primary cause of death on death certificates. However, exposure to high temperatures has wide-ranging effects on the human body, including the strain of keeping cool. This effort places pressure on the heart and kidneys . As a result, heat extremes can worsen health risks from chronic conditions and cause acute kidney injury. Researchers have linked heat to increased mortality from respiratory and cardiovascular diseases, as well as dementia and Alzheimerâ€™s .&nbsp; As a result, public health authorities and scientists cannot depend on death certificates for a full count of heat-related deaths. They instead estimate heat deaths using a number of different approaches, each with assumptions baked into their calculations.&nbsp; Dr Garyfallos Konstantinoudis , who researches methods for calculating excess mortality due to extreme events at the Grantham Institute for Climate Change and the Environment at Imperial College , tells Carbon Brief there is â€œno ground truthâ€ when it comes to tallying heat-related deaths:&nbsp; â€œWe donâ€™t know what the heat-related deaths are, so we rely on different models to describe the picture.â€ This makes the study of deaths from heatwaves similar to those from air pollution, he says: â€œThis sort of health-impact assessment has been done for years on studies related to deaths from air pollution, which have the same problem. Air pollution, until very recently , was not recorded on death certificates.&nbsp; â€œ[However], for air pollution, the [scientific] literature is much larger, so no one questions that air pollution is toxic and kills. This sort of messaging for heat is more recent.â€ There are, broadly speaking, two approaches to calculating deaths during a heatwave.&nbsp; The first involves counting the number of excess deaths relative to a period in the past.&nbsp; This method â€“ often referred to as an â€œexcess deathsâ€ approach â€“ looks at how many people died during a particular time period compared to a baseline period where there was no heatwave.&nbsp; To do this, public health authorities and researchers rely on official death figures reported by country authorities.&nbsp; The heat death tolls published in recent weeks by public health agencies in Belgium , France , Germany and the Netherlands relied on this approach.&nbsp; (For more, see: What are the pros and cons of the â€˜excess deathsâ€™ method? ) The second method uses long-term mortality data to understand the statistical relationship between temperature and mortality in a given place. The model that emerges can be used to infer the number of deaths from a heatwave in that place.&nbsp; In a rapid analysis published this week, researchers at Imperial College London , the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine (LSHTM) and the Met Office used this approach to estimate that the May and June heatwaves in the UK caused the deaths of 2,700 people. Dr Callahan â€“ working with Prof Andrew Dessler , director of the Texas Center for Extreme Weather at Texas A&M University â€“ used this method to estimate that more than 2,700 people had died in France over a 17-day period in June in an analysis for Carbon Brief .&nbsp; (For more: see: What are the pros and cons of the â€˜statistical modellingâ€™ method? ) The majority of the figures released in the wake of Europeâ€™s June heatwave have relied on these two methods. There is a third way to calculate heat deaths, which is to look at official counts of deaths attributed on death certificates to heatstroke. Callahan tells Carbon Brief that the â€œdeath-certificate codingâ€ appears to have fallen out of favour in Europe â€“ which he says is a â€œsmart moveâ€ given that it does not provide a â€œfull accountingâ€.&nbsp; Nevertheless, some public health authorities are still using this method. For example, in the wake of the heatwave in the US earlier this month, public health data showed 29 people in New Jersey and three people in New York had died from â€œheat-related illnessesâ€. Scientists tell Carbon Brief the excess deaths and statistical modelling approaches both have advantages and drawbacks. These are explored below. What do the latest figures show for Europeâ€™s May and June heatwaves? The table below shows the death numbers that have been reported by governments and researchers for Europeâ€™s May and June heatwaves, including the approach used to reach the figures. It suggests that multiple countries in Europe experienced more than 1,000 heat-related deaths during the late June heatwave, with authorities in Germany counting more than 5,000. Meanwhile, the EuroMoMo mortality monitoring system estimated there were more than 10,500 excess deaths across 27 countries during the June heatwave.&nbsp; Reported Source Country / region Dates Days Deaths Link Approach 28/06/2026 Public Health France â€‹ France 22-27 June 6 1,000 santepubliquefrance.fr Excess deaths 29/06/2026 World Health Organization Europe 21-28 June 8 1,300 x.com/DrTedros/status Excess deaths 01/07/2026 Carlos III Health Institute (MoMo) Spain 1-30 June 30 1,033 dw.com Excess deaths (all-cause and temperature-attributable) 02/07/2026 National Institute for Public Health and the Environment Netherlands 22-28 June 7 480 rivm.nl Excess deaths 03/07/2026 Public Health France â€‹ France 22-28 June 7 2,025 santepubliquefrance.fr Excess deaths 07/07/2026 Chris Callahan/Andrew Dessler France 12-29 June 18 2,766 carbonbrief.org Statistical modelling 08/07/2026 Chris Callahan Europe 15-28 June 14 13,975 zenodo.org Statistical modelling 08/07/2026 Sciensano Belgium 18 June - 1 July 14 1,747 brusselstimes.com Excess deaths 09/07/2026 Robert Koch Institute Germany 22-28 June 7 5,120 rki.de Statistical modelling 13/07/2026 Met Office/LSHTM/Imperial England and Wales 22-27 June 6 2,183 drive.google.com Statistical modelling 13/07/2026 Met Office/LSHTM/Imperial England and Wales 24-26 May 3 553 drive.google.com Statistical modelling 13/07/2026 EURO Mo/Mo 27 European countries 22-28 June 7 10,650 reuters.com Excess deaths 07/07/2025 National Institute for Public Health and the Environment Netherlands 22-28 June 7 577 archive.ph Excess deaths 14/07/2026 Germany Federal Statistical Office (Destatis) Germany 22-28 June 7 5,753 destatis.de Excess deaths In most instances, Carbon Brief has linked to the figures published by public health authorities, where numbers were first reported. In some instances, figures were released on dashboards or webpages that are updated weekly. In these cases, Carbon Brief has linked to media reports or archived web content.&nbsp; What are the pros and cons of the â€˜excess deathsâ€™ method? The excess deaths approach looks at how many more people died during a particular time period compared to a baseline period of the same length.&nbsp; For instance, on 14 July, Germanyâ€™s federal statistics agency, Destatis, published figures showing Germany saw 32% more deaths than the average in the week of 22-28 June, which was dominated by the heatwave. Specifically, the agency said that 23,932 deaths had been recorded that week, compared to an average of 18,179 in that calendar week across the years 2022-25. This suggests there were 5,753 excess deaths during the heatwave week. (This was a slight increase from preliminary Destatis figures released a week earlier, covered by Bloomberg .) The Netherlands similarly calculates excess deaths by comparing death figures against an average of deaths in a similar period during unspecified â€œprevious yearsâ€. Data published by the countryâ€™s National Institute for Public Health and the Environment (RIVM) shows that, during the week of 22-28 June, an estimated 3,626 people died in total in the northern European country.&nbsp; This is 577 more deaths than the 3,049 expected at that time of year, it said. (This is a slight revision upwards from the 480 excess deaths reported on 4 July by NL Times based on preliminary figures from NVIM.) Callahan says that the excess deaths approach has the benefit of being rapid and relatively uncomplicated: â€œIt is something that public health authorities can put out fairly quickly without having to run a fancy model and do coding like the academic scientists do. It is a short-term, high-impact, rapid estimate of mortality.â€&nbsp; The drawback to the approach is that it is impossible to decipher what percentage of these â€œall-mortalityâ€ excess deaths are, in fact, heat-related.&nbsp; Imperial Collegeâ€™s Konstantinoudis notes that the public often â€œfeels more comfortableâ€ with the excess deaths approach over the statistical modelling approach because the data it is using â€“ the official death numbers â€“ is based on real-world data.&nbsp; However, he stresses that excess deaths figures are based on a series of assumptions, including the reference period picked by researchers and how the numbers are interpreted. Statisticians and researchers have to make a series of decisions, including what period to use as a comparative baseline. For example, the baseline period could be the week before a heatwave, the same week a year before â€“ or an average of the same week across multiple years in the past. If averaging mortality of a similar period across a number of previous years, they must decide how much â€œweightâ€, or influence, each year should have. They must also decide how to account for spikes in deaths during the Covid-19 pandemic years, as well as the gradual rise in average temperatures due to global warming. During the pandemic, many governments and the World Health Organization (WHO) used the excess deaths approach to count deaths. The WHO said this metric was more â€œcomparableâ€ and â€œobjectiveâ€ than relying on national reports of Covid-19 deaths, given that different countries used different criteria for this classification. A notable example of how assumptions can skew excess death figures came during this period, when the WHO estimated in 2022 that Germany had seen 195,000 excess deaths over two years of pandemic.&nbsp; However, after statisticians and epidemiologists pointed out the assumptions in the model were not suited to Germanyâ€™s demographics, the WHO retracted the figure and eventually reduced it to 122,000 and then later to 102,000. Konstantinoudis explains: â€œCovid taught us that it is complicated. Depending on the different assumptions used in the excess-mortality approach, you get different resultsâ€¦There is a scientific basis, but we should acknowledge the assumptions.â€&nbsp; What are the pros and cons of the â€˜statistical modellingâ€™ method? In the statistical modelling approach, researchers use models to determine the specific relationship between mortality and temperature for a particular location and then apply it to temperatures observed during a heatwave.&nbsp; This allows them to estimate the overall number of deaths that were caused by a heatwave.&nbsp; Previous research has revealed that, in most places of the world, there is a U-shaped response of mortality to temperature â€“ where deaths increase rapidly in cold or hot conditions as daily maximum temperatures depart further from an â€œoptimum temperatureâ€. For example, research published in Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences in 2025 found that mortality rates in France rise as daily maximum temperatures move away from approximately 20C. This is shown in the chart below. Relationship between daily high temperature and all-cause mortality rates in France, estimated using data over 2004-19. Credit: Dr Christopher Callahan, based on data and methods in Callahan et al. ( 2025 ) Indiana Universityâ€™s Callahan say this approach allows scientists to â€œformally establish a relationship between the temperature and the mortalityâ€, adding: â€œIf you do these calculations right, you can credibly say your entire estimate of mortality is heat-related.â€ Prof Antonio Gasparrini , biostatistician and epidemiologist at LSHTM, explains the method relies on â€œtimeseries models that apply relatively sophisticated statistical methods in which you â€˜smoothâ€™ trends occurring in time, so you control for long-term trends and seasonalityâ€.&nbsp; He says that these models also allow researchers to â€œremoveâ€ trends affecting mortality that are unrelated to heat â€“ for instance, the impacts of the pandemic. They can also â€œaddâ€ other information, such as around how air pollution combines with heat to threaten vulnerable populations.&nbsp;&nbsp; Gasparrini adds: â€œWhat statistical modelling can bring is that it is more refined. It can link specific temperatures to specific impacts rather than just looking at the event [in the whole]. And also, it can be localised â€“ [data] can be stratified at a fine scale and we can look at impacts at different scales. â€œSo, it is more informative. But, at the same time, of course, itâ€™s based on more assumptions than the [excess deaths approach] and, of course, needs to be checked and compared.â€&nbsp; The approach depends on a number of judgment calls from scientists and statisticians, including the years picked to underpin the model and how to capture the lag in deaths in the days and weeks after a heatwave event.&nbsp; They must also decide at what threshold to start counting deaths â€“ in other words, whether to count all deaths above the â€œoptimum temperatureâ€ or set a higher, more extreme value â€“ and whether and how to account for any adaptation to heat extremes that may have been put in place in the study area. A benefit of the statistical modelling approach is that it opens the door for being able to attribute a specific number of deaths to human-caused climate change.&nbsp; By applying the temperature-mortality curve to both the temperatures of the recent heatwave and a counterfactual world without climate change, scientists can estimate what proportion of estimated deaths only occurred because the world is warming. For instance, Imperial College, LSHTM and Met Office researchers found that 59% and 38% of heat-related deaths in the UKâ€™s May and June heatwaves, respectively, could be attributed to climate change. Their findings are shown in the chart below. Number of heat deaths in England and Wales over 21-29 May and 18-28 June attributable to climate change. Source: Barnes et al ( 2026 ). Some climate-sceptic commentators have argued that modelled estimates are hypotheses and should therefore be treated with caution. On 13 July, climate-sceptic news website GB News covered a blog post by Oxford academics that argued the figure that 2,700 people had died in the UKâ€™s May and June heatwaves was not reflected in the provisional â€œall-mortalityâ€ data put out by the UKâ€™s Office for National Statistics (ONS). Quoting the blog, GB News said: â€œModelling tells us nothing. Models explore possibilities; surveillance tells us what happened. When the two disagree, our instinct should be to investigate the data rather than simply trust the model.â€ However, Imperialâ€™s Konstantinoudis â€“ who worked on the models behind the 2,700 figure â€“ says it is important to await the UK Health and Security Agency (UKHSA) â€™s annual heat mortality report before arriving at any conclusions. He explains: â€œWhile we are entirely clear that our current findings are modelled estimates, this methodology has consistently delivered comparable results to the UKHSAâ€™s own official analyses of observed deaths for past heat events.â€ (The UKHSA report will include updated figures and estimate excess deaths from heat based on specific periods of heat in different regions, whereas the provisional ONS figures cover all national deaths during a full-week period.) Konstantinoudis says both the excess deaths and statistical modelling approaches have been the subject of extensive peer-reviewed scientific study and can provide a â€œholistic view of what is happeningâ€ when used together.&nbsp; Studies that have compared statistical modelling approaches for estimating heatwave deaths with excess death figures in the UK have found they yield broadly similar results.&nbsp; Guest post: Fungal infections are adapting to climate change â€“ and threatening public health Guest posts | 23.09.25 Explainer: What is â€˜climate anxietyâ€™? Health and Security | 07.08.25 Climate change is creating â€˜new vulnerabilitiesâ€™ for disease pandemics Health and Security | 23.07.25 More than half a trillion hours of work lost in 2023 due to â€˜heat exposureâ€™ Health and Security | 30.10.24 jQuery(document).ready(function() { jQuery('.block-related-articles-slider-block_2c1bb764666ece639a236c19b2faa4d4 .mh').matchHeight({ byRow: false }); }); The post Q&A: Europeâ€™s May and June heatwave deaths â€“ and how they were counted appeared first on Carbon Brief .ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;1cO3_DPqbVunj3-MSYHJNI9jv_tymVo_r2RMx9VWegoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;t1-E6C71a9XH82xK2QFtVoJ-rel_wEPMjqeiD12v-JAƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bp0WeXs9vbGT8Y-moXVRBIoub7CasH6x2vKbHHiC-vcƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;CpOghOCUTDNJ_xUCCKy9lGHdV-6KOMAA26eI3UX2zD0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;yUdeLIWIk22FQchIi-b3iZuQWYSHbuAXPDxJ7aZkWvcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;M15bPkkt496cD9Y3a8HRuCM3lvinYNfM5nmXKv_rVY8ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;0jJGh4e6kLp1kp0shxrGtuZRDAE8wA2i8FRifJMZiyIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkdTextƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Cr91lc3i1S7S4SrFfExOJhP6Z_2D_tXcnbvMppb09EEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkqSyndicationSourceƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;OICg3mM8o0VTKJXRa0RPgOgjTpowPUzRp8GiG7bDOZQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx(urn:rmn:predicate:source-reports-summaryƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;4Hz4OHSr2NAnPfnSL3Lnx_b2wf-8F1YLZ7oVMl-fLmEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarktUnsaturatedPredicateƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;VYVmKIbFmePr-658WswCa64D1s2V7Sx5oxeSMioO4icƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarksGroundedPredicationƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;IgIOkRJv9EXYMp9xULHBZ5SLs5A6T-AZUK_uPMDtXoIƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;KbcDt9aD_6dvgaWNxrvx-tLM6e-soFUPNTuuH6M9Ghgƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;PWXHXrU71y2rekWxwAndWhEJatahRwARUFpkuUbm2Woƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-20T23:57:20.601Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;m6eFpwd0IxN10GQH80SL_AfCEFB4U3kx7HtLNxfTxrUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx^https://www.carbonbrief.org/qa-europes-may-and-june-heatwave-deaths-and-how-they-were-counted/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;oDXiJVdMSVuof67Z2tv9P2ZITwDgDHQ8w_o9355YNY4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;pT7nzv3TftoVQQpxdPF8T1vq-T-vmjz-oYMG-HTF5SIƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jW-zs9YVPVesOijIoWdr_ifTEmin4At8eo7h2n4L4k8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx^https://www.carbonbrief.org/qa-europes-may-and-june-heatwave-deaths-and-how-they-were-counted/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;FkR60RD3wfm2sJT36BTYxEj25rQGS7hcvllV-z0RNIMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxFri, 17 Jul 2026 11:17:09 +0000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ifbMwD9r0ebc6iRkOH9Qvc0QvQGkmLhnpcu2nZdmPtQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;MK7Nfp0ktKRIwifq9ByPkzhD1zN7CxVqSYgVzKJ05Ugƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEnti